# ==============================================================================
# Vendored PyNaCl -- false positives
# ==============================================================================
# back in may/june both lambdas had their dependencies committed straight into
# the repo instead of being pip-installed at build time. that's long gone (the
# release workflow builds them now) and the paths below don't exist any more,
# but gitleaks scans HISTORY -- the blobs are still in the commits that added
# them, so these 14 hits are permanent until the history itself is rewritten.
#
# all 14 are gitleaks' `generic-api-key` rule firing on VARIABLE NAMES, not on
# any value. that rule looks for words like passwd / secret / key / token sat
# next to an assignment, and pynacl's source is full of them: the PASSWD_MIN
# and PASSWD_MAX constants in its argon2 modules, curve_secret_key_len in
# crypto_sign. what it reports as the "secret" is just the constant on the
# other side of the assignment.
#
# NOTE: do not write those lines out in full here, keyword-then-equals and all.
# the first version of this file did exactly that and the rule matched its own
# explanation, which is what the three 260e617b fingerprints below are.
# nothing was ever redacted here because there was never anything to redact :)
#
# see for yourself, no need to take my word for it:
#     git show 8c1167b4:lambda/startup/nacl/pwhash/argon2i.py | sed -n '25,26p'
#
# regenerate the list with:
#     gitleaks git . --report-format json --report-path /tmp/leaks.json
#     jq -r '.[] | .Fingerprint' /tmp/leaks.json | sort

